Maneuvering target tracking based on a random motion model and integrated random interacting multiple model filtering
Fengqi Yang 1, Jinshan Zhong 1, Yingting Luo ∗, Ying Zhang, Xiaojing Shen, Yunmin Zhu
College of Mathematics, Sichuan University, Chengdu, Sichuan 610064, PR China
ARTICLE INFO ABSTRACT
Communicated by Jérôme Morio
Keywords:
Random motion model Maneuvering target tracking Interacting multiple model algorithm Integrated random interacting multiple model algorithm
Traditional maneuvering target tracking algorithms assume that the target motion model is either fixed or limited in number. For high-speed and highly maneuvering targets, the tracker’s performance degrades rapidly when the model set fails to adequately encompass the maneuvering mode or when there is a substantial deviation. Therefore, we propose a novel maneuvering target tracking method based on a random motion model. This algorithm employs a random model to describe the target maneuver, which is more widely applicable than traditional algorithms and remains more stable when the target maneuver is not covered by the model set. Additionally, in cases where the model set of the Interacting Multiple Model algorithm (IMM) does not align with the actual maneuvering state, the new method exhibits a smaller tracking error compared to IMM and shows no divergence trend. Finally, we combine IMM and the random motion model to propose an Integrated Random Interacting Multiple Model algorithm (IRIMM). The performance of the IRIMM algorithm closely matches that of IMM when provided with a perfectly accurate model set and significantly improves tracking effectiveness and stability when the model is incorrect.
1. Introduction
Over the last forty years, a lot of research on maneuvering target tracking has been conducted [1–3]. For maneuvering targets, maneuverability is often affected by changes in acceleration. Consequently, a fundamental challenge in maneuvering target tracking algorithms revolves around accurately modeling maneuvering acceleration. The commonly used statistical models can be roughly divided into two categories: time-series independent models and Markov models [4]. In time-series independent models, the main assumption is that the target’s acceleration or jerk is zero or constant, using time series independent disturbances to define the target maneuver. Examples of these models include the constant velocity model (CV), constant acceleration model (CA), and constant jerk model (CJ) [5,6]. On the other hand, the Markov model is different because it assumes the target acceleration and jerk are time-varying. Markov processes can describe the changing relationship between acceleration and jerk, as seen in models such as the Singer model, the current statistical model, and the Jerk model. Due to the uncertainty of the target motion state and system model, the above single model is often insufficient in describing the complex maneuvering target motion. Therefore, the current mainstream algo
