rithm used for maneuvering model tracking is the multi-model method. Representative algorithms include the Interactive Multiple Model algorithm (IMM) [7,8], Variable Structure Multiple Model algorithm (VSMM) [9–11] and improved multiple-model algorithms based on IMM [12–16]. This type of algorithm usually consists of four steps: 1) multiple possible motion models for a given target; 2) combining, cutting, and probabilistic calculation of the models through appropriate methods; 3) estimating the target state separately based on a single model; 4) coalescing the estimation results of a single model to give the final overall estimation result. Whether it is a single-model method or a multi-model method, we assume that the target motion model is fixed or limited in number. When the model set cannot cover the maneuvering mode or when there is a large deviation, tracking performance deteriorates rapidly. However, increasing the model set in a multi-model algorithm not only increases computational complexity but also causes competition between the models, leading to a decrease in filter performance. In short, a limited number of models cannot accurately describe all the maneuvering states of the target. This is the main reason behind proposing this new
